# Words 251-300 (phrasal verbs PHR-001..050)
R = [
("PHR-001","wake up – woke up – woken up – waking up","'Wake up' is complete by itself; no 'myself'. To wake someone else, put them in the middle: 'wake me up'.","WAKE UP: your eyes go UP (open).","'Wake up!' can also mean 'pay attention to reality'.","Write a sentence about what time you wake up on weekends.",("sleep deeply","the alarm rings","open your eyes"),"wake up vs get up","Wake up = stop sleeping. Get up = leave your bed. You can wake up and stay in bed."),
("PHR-002","get up – got up – got/gotten up – getting up","'Get up' is complete by itself; no reflexive pronoun is needed.","GET UP: your body goes UP from the bed.","'Get up' also means stand up from a chair.","Write a sentence about how long you stay in bed before you get up.",("lie in bed awake","push the blanket away","stand up"),"get up vs stand up","Get up = rise from bed or from lying/sitting. Stand up = move to a standing position from sitting."),
("PHR-003","go to bed – went to bed – gone to bed – going to bed","'Go to bed' is a fixed phrase: no 'my' and no 'the'.","Fixed phrases like 'go to bed', 'go to school', 'go to work' take no article.","'Go to sleep' = start sleeping. 'Go to bed' = get into bed.","Write a sentence about what you do just before you go to bed.",("finish dinner","brush your teeth","lie down in bed"),"go to bed vs go to sleep","Go to bed = get into your bed. Go to sleep = fall asleep. You might go to bed at 10 but go to sleep at 11."),
("PHR-004","get along – got along – got/gotten along – getting along","'Get along' needs 'with' before the person.","GET ALONG: walk ALONG the same road happily.","'How are you getting along?' also means 'How are you managing?'","Write a sentence about someone you get along with very well.",("meet a new classmate","talk and share jokes","become good friends"),"get along vs get on","Same meaning: 'get along with' (US and UK) = 'get on with' (UK)."),
("PHR-005","break up – broke up – broken up – breaking up","'Break up' needs 'with' before the person.","BREAK UP: the relationship breaks into two pieces.","'You're breaking up' on a phone call = the signal is bad.","Write a neutral sentence explaining what 'break up' means.",("a couple has problems","they talk and decide","they end the relationship"),"break up vs break down","Break up = end a relationship. Break down = stop working (a car), or start crying."),
("PHR-006","make up – made up – made up – making up","When it means 'become friends again', 'make up' needs no object. Use 'make up with someone'.","MAKE UP: make the friendship go back UP.","'Make up' also means invent (make up a story) or cosmetics (make-up).","Write a sentence about how you make up with a friend after a fight.",("two friends argue","one says sorry","they are friends again"),"make up vs make up for","Make up = become friends again. Make up for = do something good to balance a mistake."),
("PHR-007","fall apart – fell apart – fallen apart – falling apart","'Because' is followed by a clause directly. 'Because of' is followed by a noun.","FALL APART: the pieces fall away from each other.","'I'm falling apart' = I'm emotionally very stressed.","Write a sentence about an old object that is falling apart.",("the old chair is weak","its joints loosen","it breaks into pieces"),"because vs because of","Because + clause (because we didn't prepare). Because of + noun (because of the rain)."),
("PHR-008","fall in love – fell in love – fallen in love – falling in love","'Fall in love' needs 'with' before the person.","You FALL IN — like falling into water: sudden and deep.","'Fall in love with' can be used for places and things too: 'I fell in love with Kerala.'","Write a sentence about a place or hobby you fell in love with.",("meet someone","spend time together","feel deep love"),"fall in love vs be in love","Fall in love = the moment it starts. Be in love = the ongoing state."),
("PHR-009","look after – looked after – looked after – looking after","He/she/it in the simple present needs -s: looks after.","LOOK AFTER: keep looking AFTER someone to keep them safe.","'Look after yourself' is a caring goodbye.","Write a sentence about who looks after you when you are ill.",("a child needs care","feed and protect them","the child is safe"),"look after vs look for","Look after = take care of. Look for = search for."),
("PHR-010","take care of – took care of – taken care of – taking care of","Use 'take care of', not 'take care about'.","TAKE CARE OF: carry the CARE of something in your hands.","'I'll take care of it' = I'll handle it, don't worry.","Write a sentence about a task you'll take care of this week.",("a job needs doing","handle it responsibly","it's done"),"take care of vs take care","Take care of = look after or handle. Take care = be careful, or a friendly goodbye."),
("PHR-011","grow up – grew up – grown up – growing up","A finished childhood uses the simple past: 'I grew up in Mumbai.'","GROW UP: you grow UP in height and in age.","'Grow up!' said sharply means 'Stop acting like a child.'","Write a sentence about where you grew up.",("be a small child","years pass","become an adult"),"grow up vs grow","Grow = get bigger (plants, numbers, people). Grow up = become an adult (only people)."),
("PHR-012","hang out – hung out – hung out – hanging out","Use 'hang out WITH' friends, not 'to'.","HANG OUT: relax and just 'hang', with no plan.","'Hangout' (noun) = a place where friends meet.","Write a sentence about where you hang out with friends.",("meet friends","chat and relax together","go home happy"),"hang out vs meet","Meet = come together, any reason. Hang out = spend relaxed, unplanned time together."),
("PHR-013","calm down – calmed down – calmed down – calming down","'Calm down' is complete by itself; no 'yourself'. To calm someone else: 'calm her down'.","CALM DOWN: bring your feelings DOWN.","'Calm down' can sound rude to an angry person. 'Let's take a moment' is gentler.","Write a sentence about what helps you calm down.",("feel angry","breathe slowly","feel peaceful again"),"calm down vs cool down","Calm down = stop being upset. Cool down = become less hot, or become calmer after anger."),
("PHR-014","cheer up – cheered up – cheered up – cheering up","With a pronoun object, it goes in the middle: 'cheer me up', not 'cheer up me'.","CHEER UP: lift the mood UP.","'Cheer up!' is said to someone who looks sad.","Write a sentence about something that always cheers you up.",("feel sad","hear a funny joke","smile again"),"cheer up vs cheer for","Cheer up = become happier. Cheer for = shout support for a team."),
("PHR-015","show up – showed up – shown/showed up – showing up","Use 'show up at' an event, and 'nine o'clock', not '9 clock'.","SHOW UP: your face SHOWS in the room.","'He didn't show up' = he didn't come, although expected.","Write a sentence about someone who showed up late.",("the party starts","someone walks in","everyone sees they came"),"show up vs show off","Show up = arrive. Show off = try to impress people."),
("PHR-016","turn up – turned up – turned up – turning up","'Turn on' starts a device. 'Turn up' makes it louder or stronger.","TURN UP: the volume number goes UP.","'Turn up' also means arrive: 'He turned up late.'","Write a sentence asking someone to turn up the volume.",("the music is too soft","turn the knob","it's louder"),"turn up vs turn on","Turn on = start (turn on the TV). Turn up = increase (turn up the volume).","Please turn on the volume, I can't hear.","Please turn up the volume, I can't hear."),
("PHR-017","drop by – dropped by – dropped by – dropping by","'Drop by' takes the place directly, with no 'in'.","DROP BY: drop in for a moment as you pass BY.","'Drop by anytime' is a warm invitation.","Write a sentence inviting a friend to drop by.",("pass a friend's house","knock and say hello","leave after a short visit"),"drop by vs drop off","Drop by = visit briefly. Drop off = leave someone or something somewhere."),
("PHR-018","catch up – caught up – caught up – catching up","Use 'catch up WITH' a person.","CATCH UP: run to catch the person UP ahead.","'Let's catch up soon!' = let's meet and share news.","Write a sentence about a friend you want to catch up with.",("fall behind","work or run faster","reach the same level"),"catch up vs catch up on","Catch up with a person. Catch up on work or news."),
("PHR-019","keep in touch – kept in touch – kept in touch – keeping in touch","'With' is needed before the person.","KEEP IN TOUCH: keep your hand on the connection.","'Stay in touch' means the same.","Write a sentence about how you keep in touch with old friends.",("friends move away","call and message regularly","the friendship stays alive"),"keep in touch vs contact","Contact = reach someone once. Keep in touch = stay in contact over time."),
("PHR-020","move in – moved in – moved in – moving in","Use 'move INTO' + the place. 'Move in' alone has no place after it.","MOVE IN: carry your life IN the door.","'Move in together' = start living with a partner.","Write a sentence about when you moved into your current home.",("pack your things","carry them into the new house","start living there"),"move in vs move into","Move in (no place after it). Move into + a place: 'We moved into a flat.'"),
("PHR-021","move out – moved out – moved out – moving out","Use 'move out OF' + the place, not 'out from'.","MOVE OUT: carry your life OUT the door.","In India, 'shift' is often used ('shift house'). Standard: 'move'.","Write a sentence about when someone in your family moved out.",("pack everything","carry it out of the house","live somewhere else"),"move out vs shift","Standard English: move out / move house. 'Shift' is common in Indian English but less clear elsewhere."),
("PHR-022","settle down – settled down – settled down – settling down","Use 'in' for a city or country, not 'at'.","SETTLE DOWN: put your roots DOWN in one place.","'Settle down, class' = become calm and quiet.","Write a sentence about where you would like to settle down.",("move around a lot","find a job and home","live calmly in one place"),"settle down vs settle","Settle = decide or pay (settle a bill). Settle down = start a calm, stable life.","He wants to settle down at Pune.","He wants to settle down in Pune."),
("PHR-023","split up – split up – split up – splitting up","'Split' has the same form in the past; 'splitted' is wrong.","SPLIT → SPLIT → SPLIT, like CUT → CUT → CUT.","'Let's split up' can also mean divide into groups to search.","Write a sentence about when a group should split up to finish faster.",("a group is together","they separate","each part goes a different way"),"split up vs break up","Both can mean end a relationship. Split up is also used for groups dividing."),
("PHR-024","make out – made out – made out – making out","Put the wh-clause straight after 'make out'.","MAKE OUT: make a shape come OUT of the blur.","Careful: in informal American English, 'make out' also means kiss passionately.","Write a sentence about something you couldn't make out in the dark.",("see something blurry","look carefully","recognise what it is"),"make out vs understand","Make out = see or hear with difficulty. Understand = know the meaning."),
("PHR-025","look forward to – looked forward to – looked forward to – looking forward to","In 'look forward to', 'to' is a preposition, so it takes -ing: 'to meeting'.","LOOK FORWARD: your eyes look FORWARD in time, excited.","'I look forward to hearing from you' is a standard email ending.","Write an email ending using 'I look forward to…'.",("hear about a trip","count the days","feel excited"),"look forward to vs wait for","Wait for = spend time until something comes. Look forward to = feel happy about something coming."),
("PHR-026","count on – counted on – counted on – counting on","Use 'count on someone to do something', not 'count on that…'.","COUNT ON: they are the number you COUNT on.","'You can count on me' is a strong promise.","Write a sentence about a friend you can always count on.",("need help","call a friend","they come"),"count on vs rely on","Same meaning. Count on is slightly more informal."),
("PHR-027","rely on – relied on – relied on – relying on","The preposition after 'rely' is 'on', not 'to'.","RELY ON: you LIE back ON someone for support.","'Reliable' = someone you can rely on.","Write a sentence about who you rely on for advice.",("have a problem","ask someone you trust","get help"),"rely on vs depend on","Rely on = trust someone to help. Depend on = need something, or be decided by it."),
("PHR-028","depend on – depended on – depended on – depending on","A singular subject in the simple present needs -s: depends.","DEPEND: things HANG ('pend') on something else, like a pendant.","'It depends' is a useful short answer when you're not sure.","Write a sentence starting with 'It depends on…'.",("ask a question","look at the conditions","the answer changes with them"),"depend on vs rely on","Depend on can mean 'be decided by' (the result depends on the weather). Rely on is only about trust."),
("PHR-029","give up on – gave up on – given up on – giving up on","'Give up on' + a person means stop believing in them. 'Give up' + a thing means stop doing it.","GIVE UP ON: let go ON someone you believed in.","'Never give up on your dreams' is a common motivational line.","Write a sentence about someone who didn't give up on you.",("someone struggles","keep believing in them","they succeed"),"give up vs give up on","Give up smoking = stop doing it. Give up on a person = stop believing they can change."),
("PHR-030","get over – got over – got/gotten over – getting over","'Get over' takes its object directly, with no 'of'.","GET OVER: climb OVER a wall of sadness.","'I can't get over it!' can also mean 'I'm amazed'.","Write a sentence about how long it took you to get over an illness.",("feel ill or sad","time and rest help","feel normal again"),"get over vs overcome","Get over = recover (informal). Overcome = defeat a difficulty (more formal)."),
("PHR-031","come around – came around – come around – coming around","Use 'come around TO' an idea.","COME AROUND: walk AROUND to the other side of the argument.","'Come around' also means visit: 'Come around for dinner.'","Write a sentence about someone who finally came around to your idea.",("someone says no","think it over","they agree"),"come around vs agree","Agree = say yes. Come around = agree slowly, after first disagreeing."),
("PHR-032","warm up to – warmed up to – warmed up to – warming up to","Use 'warm up TO' a person, not 'with'.","WARM UP TO: slowly get warmer, like the sun rising.","'Warm up' alone = exercise before sport.","Write a sentence about a person or place you slowly warmed up to.",("meet someone","slowly get to know them","begin to like them"),"warm up to vs warm up","Warm up to someone = start liking them. Warm up = prepare your body for exercise."),
("PHR-033","hit it off – hit it off – hit it off – hitting it off","'It' is fixed in the middle: hit IT off.","HIT IT OFF: like a ball hit perfectly on the first try.","Common in stories: 'We hit it off immediately.'","Write a sentence about someone you hit it off with instantly.",("meet someone new","talk easily and laugh","become friends quickly"),"hit it off vs get along","Hit it off = like each other immediately. Get along = have a friendly relationship over time."),
("PHR-034","fall out – fell out – fallen out – falling out","Use 'fall out WITH' the person, not 'from'.","FALL OUT: your friendship falls OUT of place.","'Falling-out' (noun) = a serious argument.","Write a sentence about two friends who fell out over something small.",("friends argue badly","stop talking","the friendship is damaged"),"fall out vs fall down","Fall out (with someone) = have a serious argument. Fall down = drop to the ground."),
("PHR-035","patch up – patched up – patched up – patching up","'Patch up' usually needs an object like 'things' or 'the relationship'.","PATCH UP: put a PATCH on a torn friendship.","'Patch up' also means repair something quickly: 'patch up a wall'.","Write a sentence about how two friends patched things up.",("friends fight","talk and apologise","the friendship is repaired"),"patch up vs make up","Make up = become friends again. Patch things up = repair a relationship, often partly."),
("PHR-036","team up – teamed up – teamed up – teaming up","For purpose, use 'to + verb', not 'for + verb'.","TEAM UP: form a TEAM.","'Team up with' someone = join them for a task.","Write a sentence about someone you would team up with for a project.",("have a big task","join a partner","finish it together"),"team up vs join","Join = become part of an existing group. Team up = form a new partnership for a task."),
("PHR-037","pair up – paired up – paired up – pairing up","'Pair up' means groups of two only. For bigger groups, use 'split into groups' or 'form groups'.","PAIR = two, like a pair of shoes.","Teachers say 'Pair up with the person next to you.'","Write a sentence about when a teacher asked you to pair up.",("the teacher gives a task","find one partner","work in twos"),"pair up vs group up","Pair up = make groups of two. 'Group up' is rare; say 'form groups' or 'split into groups'.","We paired up in groups of three.","We split into groups of three."),
("PHR-038","look up to – looked up to – looked up to – looking up to","'To' is needed between 'look up' and the person.","LOOK UP TO: your eyes look UP at a tall hero.","Very common when talking about role models.","Write a sentence about a person you look up to and why.",("see someone's good work","admire them","want to be like them"),"look up to vs look up","Look up to = admire. Look up = search for information (look up a word)."),
("PHR-039","look down on – looked down on – looked down on – looking down on","'Look down ON' is the phrase, and he/she needs -s: looks.","LOOK DOWN ON: your nose goes UP, your eyes go DOWN on others.","It describes snobbish, unkind behaviour.","Write a sentence about why it's wrong to look down on people.",("meet someone different","think you are better","treat them as less important"),"look down on vs look up to","Opposites. Look down on = think someone is worse than you. Look up to = admire."),
("PHR-040","bump into – bumped into – bumped into – bumping into","The particle is 'into', not 'with'.","BUMP INTO: like two people bumping shoulders on a crowded street.","It can also mean physically hit: 'I bumped into the door.'","Write a sentence about someone you bumped into unexpectedly.",("walk through the station","see a familiar face","stop and chat"),"bump into vs meet","Meet can be planned. Bump into = meet by chance."),
("PHR-041","carry out – carried out – carried out – carrying out","'Next month' is future, so the verb needs 'will'.","CARRY OUT: carry a plan OUT into the real world.","Formal: 'carry out research', 'carry out orders'.","Write a sentence about a task your team carried out successfully.",("receive instructions","do each step","the task is complete"),"carry out vs do","Do is general. Carry out = do a planned task, order or experiment (more formal)."),
("PHR-042","bring up – brought up – brought up – bringing up","'Bring' is irregular: the past is 'brought'.","BRING → BROUGHT, like THINK → THOUGHT.","'Bring up' has two meanings: raise a child, or mention a topic.","Write a sentence about a topic you'd like to bring up in a meeting.",("think of an idea","wait for the right moment","mention it to the group"),"bring up vs grow up","Parents bring up a child. The child grows up."),
("PHR-043","bring about – brought about – brought about – bringing about","'Brought' is the past of 'bring', and 'damage' is uncountable: 'a lot of damage'.","BRING ABOUT: bring a change ABOUT (around) into being.","Formal: 'bring about change', 'bring about reform'.","Write a sentence about a change technology has brought about.",("a cause exists","it takes effect","a change happens"),"bring about vs cause","Same meaning. Bring about is often used for positive or planned change."),
("PHR-044","point out – pointed out – pointed out – pointing out","The thing comes after 'point out'; the person comes after 'to'.","POINT OUT: point your finger AT something so others notice.","'I'd like to point out that…' is a polite way to raise an issue.","Write a sentence about a mistake someone pointed out to you.",("see a problem","draw attention to it","everyone notices"),"point out vs show","Show = let someone see. Point out = draw attention to something specific.","She pointed me out my mistake.","She pointed out my mistake to me."),
("PHR-045","figure out – figured out – figured out – figuring out","'Figure out' takes its object directly, with no 'about'.","FIGURE OUT: work out the FIGURES (numbers) of a problem.","'I can't figure him out' = I can't understand his behaviour.","Write a sentence about a puzzle or problem you figured out.",("face a problem","think it through","find the answer"),"figure out vs find out","Figure out = solve by thinking. Find out = discover a fact, often by asking or reading."),
("PHR-046","find out – found out – found out – finding out","'Find' is irregular: the past is 'found'.","FIND → FOUND, like BIND → BOUND.","'Find out' is used for facts, not physical objects: 'I found my keys' (not 'found out').","Write a sentence about something surprising you found out recently.",("have a question","ask or read","learn the answer"),"find vs find out","Find = locate an object. Find out = discover information."),
("PHR-047","sort out – sorted out – sorted out – sorting out","'Sort out' takes its object directly, with no 'about'.","SORT OUT: sort things into order, and the problem goes OUT.","'Sorted!' is British slang for 'done, fixed'.","Write a sentence about a problem you need to sort out this week.",("things are messy","organise them one by one","the problem is solved"),"sort out vs solve","Solve = find the answer. Sort out = organise or fix a practical problem (informal)."),
("PHR-048","work out – worked out – worked out – working out","After a verb like 'work out', use the adverb 'well', not the adjective 'good'.","WORK OUT: problems and muscles both get worked out.","Three meanings: solve (work out an answer), end well (it worked out), exercise (I work out daily).","Write a sentence about a plan that worked out well.",("have a problem","try different ideas","it ends well"),"well vs good","Good is an adjective (a good plan). Well is an adverb (it worked out well)."),
("PHR-049","come up with – came up with – come up with – coming up with","'With' is part of the phrase, and 'came' is the past of 'come'.","COME UP WITH: an idea comes UP to the surface of your mind.","'Can you come up with a better idea?' is common in meetings.","Write a sentence about an idea you came up with.",("face a problem","think creatively","a new idea appears"),"come up with vs think of","Same meaning. Come up with suggests creating something new, often under pressure."),
("PHR-050","deal with – dealt with – dealt with – dealing with","After 'how', use 'to + verb': 'how to deal with'.","DEAL → DEALT: one of the few verbs ending in -t, like FEEL → FELT.","'I'll deal with it' = I'll handle it.","Write a sentence about how you deal with stress.",("a problem arrives","take action","it's handled"),"deal with vs handle","Same meaning. Handle is slightly more formal."),
]
